const S = [
 {id:'dagon',t:'Dagon',y:1917,p:`A merchant sailor, escaped from a German sea-raider, drifts upon a vast plain of slimy sea-bottom lately heaved up from the abyss, and beholds a colossal scaled thing fling its arms about a carven monolith. Sunk in morphine, he sets down his account while something lumbers against his door.`},
 {id:'polaris',t:'Polaris',y:1918,p:`A dreamer becomes a sentry of Olathoë in elder Lomar, slumbers at his post as the squat Inutos advance, and is now held captive in a waking world which he insists is the dream.`},
 {id:'statement',t:'The Statement of Randolph Carter',y:1919,p:`Harley Warren descends into an ancient tomb in the Big Cypress Swamp with a lantern and a telephone line. Carter waits above in the moonlight. The last voice upon the wire is not Warren's.`},
 {id:'sarnath',t:'The Doom That Came to Sarnath',y:1919,p:`The men of Sarnath slaughter the silent beings of Ib and bear off the idol of their god Bokrug. A thousand years after, Sarnath is gone in a single night.`},
 {id:'jermyn',t:'Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family',y:1920,p:`Arthur Jermyn learns at last what manner of bride his explorer ancestor took in the Congo, and gives himself to the flames.`},
 {id:'ulthar',t:'The Cats of Ulthar',y:1920,p:`An aged couple who torment cats vanish after the boy Menes prays; their skeletons are found picked clean, and Ulthar passes its singular law.`},
 {id:'celephais',t:'Celephaïs',y:1920,p:`Kuranes, a London dreamer, finds again the city of his childhood visions, dies a tramp's death in the waking world, and reigns as its king for ever.`},
 {id:'nyarl',t:'Nyarlathotep',y:1920,p:`A swarthy showman comes out of Egypt and tours the cities with his electrical wonders. In his wake, civilisation crumbles into nightmare.`},
 {id:'othergods',t:'The Other Gods',y:1921,p:`Barzai the Wise ascends Hatheg-Kla to behold the gods of earth at their dancing, meets instead the Other Gods, and falls screaming into the sky. His disciple Atal comes down alone.`},
 {id:'zann',t:'The Music of Erich Zann',y:1921,p:`A student lodging in the Rue d'Auseil hears the mute violist Erich Zann playing frantic music at his high garret window—music which holds back whatever lies beyond it.`},
 {id:'nameless',t:'The Nameless City',y:1921,p:`An explorer crawls into a forgotten city of the Arabian desert, built for a reptilian race and never for men, and learns at length the meaning of Alhazred's couplet.`},
 {id:'outsider',t:'The Outsider',y:1921,p:`A lonely being climbs out of a lightless castle into a blazing ballroom, sees the revellers flee, and touches the monster in the gilded frame.`},
 {id:'west',t:'Herbert West—Reanimator',y:1922,p:`A medical student reanimates the dead from Arkham to Flanders, until his creations come back for him.`},
 {id:'hound',t:'The Hound',y:1922,p:`Two grave-robbers steal a jade amulet from a Dutch grave and are hunted by a faint, far baying. Here the Necronomicon is named for the first time.`},
 {id:'rats',t:'The Rats in the Walls',y:1923,p:`Delapore restores Exham Priory, follows the scurrying in its walls down into a vast twilit grotto, and descends at last into his ancestors.`},
 {id:'unnamable',t:'The Unnamable',y:1923,p:`In an ancient Arkham burying-ground, Randolph Carter disputes with Joel Manton whether anything can truly be "unnamable"—until something answers.`},
 {id:'festival',t:'The Festival',y:1923,p:`A man comes to ancient Kingsport for the Yuletide rite of his forebears, and descends beneath the church to an oily subterranean river and the winged things that bear the worshippers away.`},
 {id:'coc',t:'The Call of Cthulhu',y:1926,p:`Francis Thurston assembles his late grand-uncle's notes upon a dream-born sculpture, a Louisiana swamp-cult, and a sailor's account of the risen city of R'lyeh.`},
 {id:'pickman',t:"Pickman's Model",y:1926,p:`Thurber explains why he dropped the painter Richard Upton Pickman—and what was in the photograph pinned to the canvas.`},
 {id:'silverkey',t:'The Silver Key',y:1926,p:`At fifty, disenchanted with the world, Randolph Carter finds an ancestral silver key, returns to the scenes of his boyhood near Arkham, and vanishes from the waking world.`},
 {id:'strangehouse',t:'The Strange High House in the Mist',y:1926,p:`The philosopher Thomas Olney climbs to the house upon Kingsport's loftiest cliff, and comes down with his soul left behind him. Nodens passes overhead.`},
 {id:'kadath',t:'The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath',y:1927,p:`Randolph Carter crosses the Dreamlands in quest of the sunset city the gods have denied him—from Ulthar to the moon, through the underworld, to Leng and to Kadath itself.`},
 {id:'cdw',t:'The Case of Charles Dexter Ward',y:1927,p:`A young antiquarian raises his wizard ancestor Joseph Curwen, who murders and replaces him. The family physician goes down into the catacombs.`},
 {id:'colour',t:'The Colour Out of Space',y:1927,p:`A meteorite falls upon Nahum Gardner's farm west of Arkham, and something within it slowly drains the land and the family to grey dust.`},
 {id:'yig',t:'The Curse of Yig',y:1928,rev:'with Zealia Bishop',p:`In the Oklahoma of 1889, Walker Davis kills a nest of rattlesnakes. His wife Audrey waits in the dark for the vengeance of the serpent-god.`},
 {id:'dunwich',t:'The Dunwich Horror',y:1928,p:`Wilbur Whateley, son of an unnamed father, dies in the attempt to steal the Necronomicon, and his invisible twin breaks loose across the Dunwich hills.`},
 {id:'mound',t:'The Mound',y:1930,rev:'with Zealia Bishop',p:`An Oklahoma mound hides the manuscript of Zamacona, a Spanish conquistador who passed into the blue-litten underground world of K'n-yan.`},
 {id:'whisperer',t:'The Whisperer in Darkness',y:1930,p:`The folklorist Albert Wilmarth corresponds with Henry Akeley concerning winged things in the Vermont hills—and then pays him a visit.`},
 {id:'mountains',t:'At the Mountains of Madness',y:1931,p:`The geologist William Dyer explains why none should follow the Miskatonic expedition to the Antarctic: the city beyond the mountains, and what still lives beneath it.`},
 {id:'innsmouth',t:'The Shadow over Innsmouth',y:1931,p:`A young traveller passes through the decayed port of Innsmouth, flees its hybrid folk by night, and discovers whose blood he carries.`},
 {id:'witch',t:'The Dreams in the Witch House',y:1932,p:`The mathematics student Walter Gilman takes Keziah Mason's old garret, and dreams through its angles into other dimensions as Walpurgis Night draws near.`},
 {id:'museum',t:'The Horror in the Museum',y:1932,rev:'with Hazel Heald',p:`Stephen Jones wagers that he can pass a night in George Rogers's waxworks, where Rogers keeps the god he brought back from Alaska.`},
 {id:'gate',t:'Through the Gates of the Silver Key',y:1933,rev:'with E. Hoffmann Price',p:`Carter's heirs meet in New Orleans to divide his estate, and a veiled swami relates what lay beyond the Ultimate Gate.`},
 {id:'aeons',t:'Out of the Aeons',y:1933,rev:'with Hazel Heald',p:`A mummy in Boston's Cabot Museum, and the scroll found with it, tell of T'yog of Mu, who climbed Yaddith-Gho to defy Ghatanothoa.`},
 {id:'doorstep',t:'The Thing on the Doorstep',y:1933,p:`Daniel Upton explains why he has sent six bullets through the head of his best friend, Edward Derby.`},
 {id:'shadowtime',t:'The Shadow Out of Time',y:1935,p:`Professor Nathaniel Peaslee loses five years to a strange amnesia, then finds proof in the Australian desert that his mind had been elsewhere.`},
 {id:'haunter',t:'The Haunter of the Dark',y:1935,p:`The writer Robert Blake explores a deserted church on Federal Hill, finds the Shining Trapezohedron, and wakes what it summons.`},
];

/* ---------- Entries ---------- */
const E = [
/* Other Gods */
{id:'azathoth',n:'Azathoth',c:'og',ep:'The daemon sultan at the centre of infinity',s:['kadath','whisperer','witch','haunter'],l:['nyarlathotep','keziah','gilman','blake','kadath','shantaks'],
 d:`Lovecraft's supreme power is a blind and mindless one. Azathoth sprawls at the black centre of Ultimate Chaos, gnawing hungrily beyond time, while the lesser Other Gods dance about it to the thin, monotonous piping of daemoniac flutes. It does not scheme; Nyarlathotep is its soul and messenger. It was to that nighted throne that Keziah Mason meant to bring Walter Gilman, there to sign the book in his own blood.`,
 q:`the boundless daemon sultan Azathoth, whose name no lips dare speak aloud`,qs:'kadath'},
{id:'yogsothoth',n:'Yog-Sothoth',c:'og',ep:'The Key and the Gate',s:['cdw','dunwich','whisperer','haunter','museum','gate'],l:['wilbur','twin','lavinia','oldwhateley','curwen','ward','carter','umr','necronomicon','sentinelhill','rogers','silverkey'],
 d:`Yog-Sothoth touches every point of time and space at once, and that is why sorcerers covet it: whosoever has its favour may pass anywhere and anywhen. Joseph Curwen's formulae call upon it, and on Candlemas of 1913 Lavinia Whateley bore it twin sons. George Rogers's waxwork depicts it as a congeries of iridescent globes. Beyond the Ultimate Gate, Randolph Carter meets it at last as the All-in-One.`,
 q:`Yog-Sothoth knows the gate. Yog-Sothoth is the gate. Yog-Sothoth is the key and guardian of the gate.`,qs:'dunwich'},
{id:'nyarlathotep',n:'Nyarlathotep',c:'og',ep:'The Crawling Chaos',s:['nyarl','kadath','whisperer','witch','haunter'],l:['azathoth','carter','blake','trapezohedron','starrywisdom','gilman','keziah','migo','greatones','kadath','akeley','shantaks'],
 d:`Alone among the Other Gods he walks among men, and seems to relish it. In the prose-poem of 1920 he comes out of Egypt, a swarthy showman of electrical marvels, and the world unravels in the wake of his tour. He is the tall, smiling pharaoh who toys with Randolph Carter upon Kadath; the Black Man of the Arkham witch-cult; the Mighty Messenger of the Mi-Go; and the winged thing which the Shining Trapezohedron calls down upon Robert Blake.`,
 q:`And it was then that Nyarlathotep came out of Egypt.`,qs:'nyarl'},
{id:'shub',n:'Shub-Niggurath',c:'og',ep:'The Black Goat of the Woods with a Thousand Young',s:['dunwich','whisperer','mound','aeons'],l:['migo','akeley','wilmarth','tyog','kenyan','oldwhateley'],
 d:`Lovecraft never shows Shub-Niggurath, yet the name runs like a refrain through every chant: on the Vermont phonograph record, in the Dunwich rites, in the temples of K'n-yan. In "Out of the Aeons" the priest T'yog serves it upon Mu and draws upon its aid against Ghatanothoa. Its very title hints at some monstrous fecundity—a mother of endless young.`,
 q:`Iä! Shub-Niggurath! The Black Goat of the Woods with a Thousand Young!`,qs:'whisperer'},

/* Great Old Ones & deities */
{id:'cthulhu',n:'Cthulhu',c:'gd',ep:"Dead but dreaming in R'lyeh",s:['coc','innsmouth','whisperer','mountains','mound'],l:['rlyeh','thurston','angell','legrasse','johansen','wilcox','castro','cthulhucult','starspawn','deepones','dagon','necronomicon','kenyan'],
 d:`Mountainous, with an octopus's head, a scaly body and rudimentary wings, the high priest of the Great Old Ones lies dead but dreaming in sunken R'lyeh until the stars come right, and his dreams seep into sensitive minds. In the spring of 1925 R'lyeh rose and he came forth. Gustaf Johansen drove the steam yacht Alert full through him; the gelatinous mass recombined, and the city sank once more. In K'n-yan he is worshipped as Tulu. The chant, rendered into English, runs: "In his house at R'lyeh dead Cthulhu waits dreaming."`,
 q:`Ph'nglui mglw'nafh Cthulhu R'lyeh wgah'nagl fhtagn.`,qs:'coc'},
{id:'dagon',n:'Dagon',c:'gd',ep:'Father Dagon',s:['dagon','innsmouth'],l:['hydra','deepones','eod','obed','innsmouth','yhanthlei','cthulhu'],
 d:`In "Dagon" a sailor, adrift upon a newly risen plain of sea-bottom, watches a gigantic scaled creature rise from the water and fling its arms about a carven monolith. In "The Shadow over Innsmouth" Father Dagon is the god of the Deep Ones, worshipped together with Mother Hydra by the Esoteric Order of Dagon.`,
 q:`The end is near. I hear a noise at the door, as of some immense slippery body lumbering against it.`,qs:'dagon'},
{id:'hydra',n:'Mother Hydra',c:'gd',ep:'Consort of Dagon',s:['innsmouth'],l:['dagon','deepones','eod','zadok'],
 d:`Named in the ravings of old Zadok Allen and in the oaths of the Esoteric Order, Mother Hydra stands beside Father Dagon at the head of the Deep Ones. Of her form Lovecraft tells us nothing whatever.`},
{id:'tsathoggua',n:'Tsathoggua',c:'gd',ep:'Borrowed from Clark Ashton Smith',s:['whisperer','mound'],l:['kenyan','zamacona','wilmarth','akeley','eibon'],
 d:`Smith devised this sluggish, toad-like god, and Lovecraft wrote him into his own fiction. Wilmarth recognises the name in Akeley's letters. In "The Mound", K'n-yan once worshipped Tsathoggua, until explorers went down into black N'kai beneath their world and found his images tended by living, formless black things. After that they abandoned the cult.`},
{id:'yig',n:'Yig',c:'gd',ep:'Father of Serpents',s:['yig','mound'],l:['kenyan','zamacona'],
 d:`A serpent-god of the southern Plains who avenges any harm done to his children. In 1889 Walker Davis killed a nest of rattlesnakes; and his wife Audrey, certain that the curse was coming, took an axe to the shape she saw in the dark—and it was her husband. What she bore afterward is kept at the asylum still. In "The Mound" Yig is one of K'n-yan's chief gods.`},
{id:'ghatanothoa',n:'Ghatanothoa',c:'gd',ep:'The god of Yaddith-Gho',s:['aeons'],l:['tyog','migo','yuggoth','shub'],
 d:`The fungous beings from Yuggoth brought Ghatanothoa to earth, and it lairs in the fortress beneath Mount Yaddith-Gho upon Mu. To behold it, or even a faithful image of it, is to be turned to leathery stone with the brain still living within. T'yog climbed the mountain bearing a scroll to overthrow it; but its priests had exchanged the scroll.`},
{id:'rhantegoth',n:'Rhan-Tegoth',c:'gd',ep:'The god in the waxworks',s:['museum'],l:['rogers','yuggoth'],
 d:`George Rogers claimed to have found this god dormant upon an ivory throne amid Arctic ruins in Alaska. It has a globular body, six long limbs ending in crab-like claws, and a head with a sucking proboscis. It came down from Yuggoth three million years ago, and Rogers fed it.`},
{id:'bokrug',n:'Bokrug',c:'gd',ep:'The great water-lizard',s:['sarnath'],l:['sarnath'],
 d:`The god of the silent green beings of Ib, in the land of Mnar. When the men of Sarnath massacred them, they bore off his sea-green stone idol as a trophy. A thousand years later, at the feast which celebrated the destruction of Ib, Sarnath was blotted out in a single night. Only the idol remained.`},
{id:'nodens',n:'Nodens',c:'gd',ep:'Lord of the Great Abyss',s:['strangehouse','kadath'],l:['nightgaunts','carter','kingsport'],
 d:`A hoary and archaic god of the deep, who rides a vast shell through the sky above Kingsport. He is one of the few powers in Lovecraft that aid mankind: the night-gaunts serve him, and in the Dream-Quest they bear Randolph Carter out of peril.`},
{id:'greatones',n:'The Great Ones',c:'gd',ep:'The gods of Earth',s:['othergods','kadath'],l:['kadath','barzai','atal','nyarlathotep','carter'],
 d:`The mild gods of earth have withdrawn to an onyx castle upon unknown Kadath, and dance upon the peaks on moonlit nights. They are weak, and the Other Gods and Nyarlathotep protect them. Barzai climbed Hatheg-Kla to behold them and was taken by the Other Gods; Carter crosses the whole of the Dreamlands to petition them.`},
{id:'umr',n:"'Umr at-Tawil",c:'gd',ep:'The Most Ancient One',s:['gate'],l:['yogsothoth','carter','silverkey','necronomicon'],
 d:`The veiled guide at the Ultimate Gate. He receives Randolph Carter beyond the first gateway and leads him past the Ancient Ones upon their pedestals toward Yog-Sothoth. The Necronomicon renders his name as "the Prolonged of Life."`},

/* Beings & races */
{id:'elderthings',n:'Elder Things',c:'br',ep:'The Old Ones of Antarctica',s:['mountains','witch'],l:['shoggoths','dyer','danforth','lake','antarctic','starspawn','migo','gilman'],
 d:`Barrel-bodied, eight feet in length, with folding membranous wings, a five-pointed starfish head, and a fivefold symmetry throughout. They came to the young earth from space, reared a titanic city upon the Antarctic plateau, and may by accident have created terrestrial life. They warred with Cthulhu's spawn and with the Mi-Go. Lake's expedition dug up specimens which were not dead. Walter Gilman brings one of their images back out of a dream.`,
 q:`Radiates, vegetables, monstrosities, star-spawn—whatever they had been, they were men!`,qs:'mountains'},
{id:'shoggoths',n:'Shoggoths',c:'br',ep:'Servants of the Elder Things',s:['mountains','doorstep'],l:['elderthings','dyer','danforth','antarctic','derby'],
 d:`Fifteen-foot masses of black, iridescent protoplasm, bred by the Elder Things as living tools and moulded into new organs at hypnotic command. Across the aeons they grew minds of their own, learned to mimic their masters' piping speech, and rebelled. Dyer and Danforth flee one through the tunnels beneath the city as it comes on like a subway train. Edward Derby raves of the pit of the shoggoths.`,
 q:`Tekeli-li! Tekeli-li!`,qs:'mountains'},
{id:'tindalos',n:'Hounds of Tindalos',c:'br',ep:'Borrowed from Frank Belknap Long',s:['whisperer'],l:['wilmarth','akeley','migo','yig'],
 d:`Long devised these lean hunters, which stalk their prey through the angles of time, in "The Hounds of Tindalos" (1929). Lovecraft returned the compliment with a single line in "The Whisperer in Darkness": among the cosmic secrets poured out to Wilmarth, he learns "the essence (though not the source)" of the Hounds. That lone mention is the whole of their part in Lovecraft's own fiction.`},
{id:'migo',n:'Mi-Go',c:'br',ep:'The Fungi from Yuggoth',s:['whisperer','mountains'],l:['yuggoth','akeley','wilmarth','nyarlathotep','shub','elderthings','ghatanothoa'],
 d:`Pinkish beings some five feet long, part crustacean and part fungus, with several pairs of limbs, membranous wings that beat through the ether, and a convoluted head that shifts in colour as they converse. From their outpost upon Yuggoth they mine the Vermont hills, imitate human voices with a buzzing drone, and bear living human brains to the stars in metal cylinders. The Himalayan legends of the Mi-Go concern them.`},
{id:'yith',n:'Great Race of Yith',c:'br',ep:'Minds that travel through time',s:['shadowtime'],l:['peaslee','polyps','yithcity','pnakotic'],
 d:`Minds which fled their dying world of Yith and seized upon ten-foot, cone-shaped bodies on earth some 150 million years ago. They exchange minds with beings throughout time to study them, while the displaced mind dwells in a cone-body and writes its history for their archive. Before the polyps destroyed them, their minds fled once again, into a beetle-like race that will follow mankind.`},
{id:'polyps',n:'Flying Polyps',c:'br',ep:'The elder enemies of the Great Race',s:['shadowtime'],l:['yith','peaslee','yithcity'],
 d:`Half-material beings, partly invisible, which moved without wings and struck with blasts of wind, heralded by a peculiar whistling. The Great Race drove them into basalt cities underground and sealed the trapdoors. The polyps broke out. Peaslee finds one of those trapdoors open.`},
{id:'deepones',n:'Deep Ones',c:'br',ep:'Children of Dagon',s:['innsmouth'],l:['dagon','hydra','cthulhu','obed','zadok','olmstead','yhanthlei','innsmouth','eod','asenath'],
 d:`Grey-green amphibians with pale bellies, gills, bulging unwinking eyes and croaking voices. They dwell in cities beneath the sea, cannot die save by violence, and breed with humankind; their offspring slowly acquire the "Innsmouth look" and at length go down to the water for good. Obed Marsh's bargain gave Innsmouth fish and gold in exchange for its people.`},
{id:'starspawn',n:'Star-spawn of Cthulhu',c:'br',ep:"Builders of R'lyeh",s:['coc','mountains'],l:['cthulhu','rlyeh','elderthings'],
 d:`The octopoid race that came down from the stars with Cthulhu. Dyer reads their deep history in the carvings of the Elder Things: they warred with the Old Ones and held the Pacific lands, until R'lyeh sank and sealed them in.`},
{id:'colour',n:'The Colour Out of Space',c:'br',ep:'A hue outside the spectrum',s:['colour'],l:['nahum','ammi','blastedheath','miskatonic'],
 d:`In June of 1882 a meteorite fell beside Nahum Gardner's well. It shrank away under the tests of the Miskatonic professors, and held a globule of a colour that belonged to no known part of the spectrum. Something settled in the well. The crops swelled and turned bitter, beasts and people crumbled into grey dust, and part of the thing shot back into the sky. The rest remained.`,
 q:`It was just a colour out of space—a frightful messenger from unformed realms of infinity beyond all Nature as we know it.`,qs:'colour'},
{id:'twin',n:"Wilbur's Twin",c:'br',ep:'The Dunwich Horror',s:['dunwich'],l:['wilbur','yogsothoth','armitage','sentinelhill','dunwich','lavinia','oldwhateley'],
 d:`Raised unseen in the boarded-up Whateley farmhouse and fed upon cattle, it outgrew the house. After Wilbur's death it broke loose, flattening farms and leaving prints the size of barrel-heads. Armitage's powder made it visible upon Sentinel Hill: a heaving mass of ropy tentacles, larger than a barn, with a half-face like Wilbur's at the top. It screamed for its father before it went.`,
 q:`HELP! HELP! ...ff—ff—ff—FATHER! FATHER! YOG-SOTHOTH!`,qs:'dunwich'},
{id:'brownjenkin',n:'Brown Jenkin',c:'br',ep:"Keziah Mason's familiar",s:['witch'],l:['keziah','gilman','arkham'],
 d:`A rat-sized, furry thing with sharp teeth, a bearded and evil human face, and tiny human hands. It bore messages between Keziah and the Devil, and nursed upon the witch's blood. It finished Walter Gilman by eating its way out of his chest.`},
{id:'kenyan',n:"People of K'n-yan",c:'br',ep:'The realm beneath Oklahoma',s:['mound'],l:['zamacona','tsathoggua','yig','shub','cthulhu'],
 d:`An ancient, telepathic people dwelling in a blue-litten cavern-world beneath Oklahoma. They can dematerialise at will, have all but conquered death, and have sunk into idle cruelty, served by slaves and by the half-human gyaa-yothn. They worship Tulu and Yig. The conquistador Zamacona lived among them, and sought to escape.`},

/* Dreamlands */
{id:'ghouls',n:'Ghouls',c:'dl',ep:'Feeders in the dark',s:['pickman','outsider','kadath'],l:['pickman','carter','nightgaunts','gugs','ghasts','outsider','moonbeasts'],
 d:`Rubbery, dog-faced, half-hoofed eaters of the dead, who burrow beneath graveyards both in the waking world and in the dream-underworld. Some were once human: Richard Upton Pickman painted them, and afterward joined them. In the Dream-Quest they are Carter's allies, and their army storms the rock of the moon-beasts.`},
{id:'nightgaunts',n:'Night-gaunts',c:'dl',ep:'Faceless servants of Nodens',s:['kadath'],l:['nodens','ghouls','carter','shantaks'],
 d:`Black, slick, faceless things with inward-curving horns, barbed tails and silent bat-wings. They do not bite; they seize, tickle, and bear their victims away. They serve Nodens and keep a pact with the ghouls, who lend them to Carter. Lovecraft dreamed of them as a child.`},
{id:'gugs',n:'Gugs',c:'dl',ep:'Giants of the underworld',s:['kadath'],l:['ghouls','ghasts','carter'],
 d:`Hairy giants twenty feet high, whose mouths open vertically and whose arms each divide into two forearms at the elbow. The Great Ones banished them underground for their abominable rites. The ghouls are at feud with them, and Carter creeps past their sleeping city.`},
{id:'ghasts',n:'Ghasts',c:'dl',ep:'Hoppers of the vaults of Zin',s:['kadath'],l:['gugs','ghouls'],
 d:`Things that leap like kangaroos, with faces disturbingly near to human but without noses. Strong light kills them. They hunt gugs in the vaults of Zin.`},
{id:'moonbeasts',n:'Moon-beasts',c:'dl',ep:'The pale toads of the Moon',s:['kadath'],l:['lengmen','cats','carter','ghouls'],
 d:`Great greyish-white toadlike things that can stretch their shape at will, with a mass of short pink tentacles upon their snouts. They sail black galleys to the dream-ports, trading rubies for slaves, and are served by the men of Leng. They capture Carter, and are routed by the cats of Ulthar.`},
{id:'lengmen',n:'Men of Leng',c:'dl',ep:'The horned merchants',s:['kadath'],l:['moonbeasts','leng','carter'],
 d:`Almost-men, with wide mouths, small horns and hooves, who hide beneath turbans and shoes to trade in the harbours of the Dreamlands. They dwell in stone villages upon the plateau of Leng, and are slaves to the moon-beasts.`},
{id:'zoogs',n:'Zoogs',c:'dl',ep:'Whisperers of the Enchanted Wood',s:['kadath'],l:['cats','carter'],
 d:`Small, brown, furtive creatures that dwell in burrows in the Enchanted Wood and know many secrets of the dream-world. They love the wine of the moon-tree, and plot against the cats of Ulthar, which catch them at it.`},
{id:'shantaks',n:'Shantaks',c:'dl',ep:'The horse-headed birds',s:['kadath'],l:['nightgaunts','nyarlathotep','carter','leng'],
 d:`Birds larger than elephants, with horse-like heads and slippery scales in place of feathers, which nest near Leng. They fear only the night-gaunts. Nyarlathotep sends Carter off upon one, toward Azathoth.`},
{id:'cats',n:'The Cats of Ulthar',c:'dl',ep:"Lovecraft's heroes",s:['ulthar','kadath'],l:['ulthar','carter','zoogs','moonbeasts','atal'],
 d:`No man in Ulthar may kill a cat, ever since an old couple who murdered them were found picked to the bone after the boy Menes prayed. In the Dream-Quest Carter can speak with cats; they save him from the zoogs, and cross to the far side of the moon to rescue him from the moon-beasts. Lovecraft, it should be said, adored them.`,
 q:`It is said that in Ulthar, which lies beyond the river Skai, no man may kill a cat.`,qs:'ulthar'},

/* People */
{id:'thurston',n:'Francis Wayland Thurston',c:'pp',ep:'Narrator of The Call of Cthulhu',s:['coc'],l:['angell','legrasse','johansen','wilcox','cthulhu'],
 d:`A Bostonian who inherits the papers of his grand-uncle George Angell, and pieces together the dream-sculpture, the Louisiana cult and Johansen's account. By the end he expects to die for what he knows.`,
 q:`The most merciful thing in the world, I think, is the inability of the human mind to correlate all its contents.`,qs:'coc'},
{id:'angell',n:'George Gammell Angell',c:'pp',ep:'Collector of the Cthulhu notes',s:['coc'],l:['thurston','wilcox','legrasse','cthulhu','cthulhucult'],
 d:`Professor Emeritus of Semitic Languages at Brown University. He heard Legrasse's story in 1908, gathered reports of the dream-wave of 1925, and interviewed Wilcox. In 1926 he collapsed after a sailor jostled him on the Providence waterfront, and Thurston suspects that he was killed.`},
{id:'legrasse',n:'John Raymond Legrasse',c:'pp',ep:'Raider of the swamp cult',s:['coc'],l:['castro','cthulhucult','angell','thurston','cthulhu'],
 d:`A New Orleans police inspector who led the raid of 1907 upon a cult ritual in the Louisiana swamps, and seized its greenish-black idol. He brought it to the 1908 meeting of the American Archaeological Society in the hope of learning what it was.`},
{id:'johansen',n:'Gustaf Johansen',c:'pp',ep:'The man who rammed Cthulhu',s:['coc'],l:['cthulhu','rlyeh','thurston','cthulhucult'],
 d:`A Norwegian second mate whose schooner Emma was set upon by the cultist crew of the Alert in 1925. The survivors took the yacht and landed upon risen R'lyeh, where Cthulhu came forth. Johansen steered straight through the god, came home to Oslo, wrote down his account, and soon died.`},
{id:'wilcox',n:'Henry Anthony Wilcox',c:'pp',ep:'The dreaming sculptor',s:['coc'],l:['angell','cthulhu'],
 d:`A young sculptor who brought Angell a bas-relief he had modelled from a dream on the 1st of March, 1925, and repeated the words "Cthulhu fhtagn." He lay in fever through the weeks in which R'lyeh stood above the waves, and woke remembering nothing once it sank.`},
{id:'castro',n:'Old Castro',c:'pp',ep:'The cultist who talked',s:['coc'],l:['cthulhucult','legrasse','cthulhu'],
 d:`A very aged prisoner from Legrasse's raid, who claimed to have met the cult's deathless leaders in the mountains of China. Nearly all that is known of the Great Old Ones and their return comes from his testimony.`},
{id:'carter',n:'Randolph Carter',c:'pp',ep:'The dreamer',s:['statement','unnamable','silverkey','kadath','gate'],l:['warren','kuranes','pickman','atal','nyarlathotep','greatones','umr','silverkey','cats','ghouls','nightgaunts','kadath','yogsothoth','arkham'],
 d:`Lovecraft's recurring dreamer, and his nearest alter ego. He waited above Harley Warren's tomb, disputed the unnamable in an Arkham burying-ground, crossed the Dreamlands to Kadath, and in 1928 vanished with his silver key. Through the Gates tells whither he went.`},
{id:'warren',n:'Harley Warren',c:'pp',ep:'The man in the tomb',s:['statement'],l:['carter'],
 d:`Carter's friend and fellow occultist, who carried a book in an unknown alphabet. He went down into an ancient tomb in the Big Cypress Swamp with a lantern and a telephone wire, while Carter remained above.`,
 q:`YOU FOOL, WARREN IS DEAD!`,qs:'statement'},
{id:'west',n:'Herbert West',c:'pp',ep:'Reanimator',s:['west'],l:['miskatonic','arkham'],
 d:`A slight, blond, spectacled medical student of Miskatonic who held that life was a chemical process which might be started anew. He tried it upon ever fresher corpses, from Arkham to Bolton to the trenches of Flanders. In the end his creations came for him, led by a headless surgeon.`},
{id:'alhazred',n:'Abdul Alhazred',c:'pp',ep:'The mad Arab',s:['nameless','hound','festival','dunwich'],l:['necronomicon','namelesscity'],
 d:`A poet of Sanaa in Yemen, who wandered among the ruins of Babylon and Memphis and passed ten years alone in the great southern desert. At Damascus, about 730, he wrote Al Azif, the book afterward called the Necronomicon. According to Lovecraft's "History of the Necronomicon," an invisible monster seized and devoured him in broad daylight in 738.`},
{id:'wilbur',n:'Wilbur Whateley',c:'pp',ep:'The goatish boy of Dunwich',s:['dunwich'],l:['lavinia','oldwhateley','twin','yogsothoth','armitage','necronomicon','miskatonic','dunwich'],
 d:`Born on Candlemas of 1913, Wilbur grew at a monstrous rate: walking at seven months, and looking a grown man by the age of ten. He needed the complete Necronomicon to finish his grandfather's work. The watchdog of the Miskatonic library killed him as he broke in to steal it, and below the waist he was not human.`},
{id:'lavinia',n:'Lavinia Whateley',c:'pp',ep:'Mother of the twins',s:['dunwich'],l:['wilbur','twin','oldwhateley','yogsothoth'],
 d:`A slightly deformed albino woman of five-and-thirty, who read her father's books and roamed the hills in thunderstorms. She bore Wilbur, and his twin, without ever naming the father. She disappeared about Hallowe'en of 1926.`},
{id:'oldwhateley',n:'Old Whateley',c:'pp',ep:'The wizard of Dunwich',s:['dunwich'],l:['wilbur','lavinia','twin','yogsothoth','shub','sentinelhill'],
 d:`Lavinia's father, a wizard whom all Dunwich feared. He rebuilt the farmhouse for what was coming and taught Wilbur the rites. Dying in 1924, he bade Wilbur keep feeding the thing upstairs, and seek the long chant on page 751 of the complete edition.`},
{id:'armitage',n:'Henry Armitage',c:'pp',ep:'Librarian of Miskatonic',s:['dunwich'],l:['wilbur','twin','necronomicon','miskatonic','sentinelhill'],
 d:`Head librarian of Miskatonic University, who refused to lend Wilbur the library's Latin Necronomicon. After Wilbur's death he deciphered the boy's diary, and with Professors Rice and Morgan climbed Sentinel Hill to destroy the twin with a powder and a counter-chant.`},
{id:'wilmarth',n:'Albert N. Wilmarth',c:'pp',ep:'The skeptical folklorist',s:['whisperer'],l:['akeley','migo','yuggoth','miskatonic','tsathoggua'],
 d:`An instructor of literature at Miskatonic, who writes sceptical rebuttals after the Vermont floods of 1927 wash strange bodies down the rivers. Henry Akeley's letters convince him. His visit to Akeley's farmhouse ends in a flight through the dark.`},
{id:'akeley',n:'Henry Wentworth Akeley',c:'pp',ep:'The besieged farmer',s:['whisperer'],l:['wilmarth','migo','shub','yuggoth','nyarlathotep'],
 d:`A scholarly recluse in the Vermont hills, who records the rites of the Mi-Go upon a phonograph and holds them off with dogs and rifles. Then his letters turn calm and welcoming. When Wilmarth arrives, Akeley's brain is in a cylinder bound for Yuggoth, and the thing in his chair had been wearing his face and hands.`},
{id:'dyer',n:'William Dyer',c:'pp',ep:'Leader of the Antarctic expedition',s:['mountains'],l:['danforth','lake','elderthings','shoggoths','antarctic','miskatonic','leng'],
 d:`The Miskatonic geologist who led the expedition of 1930–31. With Danforth he flew over the mountains and walked the dead city of the Elder Things. He writes his account to stop the Starkweather-Moore expedition from going back.`},
{id:'danforth',n:'Danforth',c:'pp',ep:'The one who looked back',s:['mountains'],l:['dyer','shoggoths','elderthings','necronomicon'],
 d:`A graduate student, widely read in forbidden books, who flew with Dyer. As they cleared the range he looked back at something beyond it, and broke. He will only hint at what he saw, and sometimes cries out the shoggoths' word.`},
{id:'lake',n:'Professor Lake',c:'pp',ep:'The biologist',s:['mountains'],l:['elderthings','dyer'],
 d:`The expedition's biologist, who led a party to the north-west and dug up fourteen star-headed specimens from a cave. His wireless reports grow ever more excited, and then cease. Dyer finds the camp torn to pieces.`},
{id:'olmstead',n:'The Innsmouth Narrator',c:'pp',ep:"Robert Olmstead, in Lovecraft's notes",s:['innsmouth'],l:['zadok','obed','deepones','innsmouth','yhanthlei','eod'],
 d:`A young man touring New England for his coming of age, who stops in Innsmouth in 1927. Having escaped the town by night, he traces his family and finds the Marsh blood. As the Innsmouth look comes upon him, he resolves to free his cousin from the asylum at Canton and swim out to Y'ha-nthlei.`,
 q:`and in that lair of the Deep Ones we shall dwell amidst wonder and glory for ever.`,qs:'innsmouth'},
{id:'zadok',n:'Zadok Allen',c:'pp',ep:'The old drunk who talked',s:['innsmouth'],l:['obed','deepones','hydra','olmstead','innsmouth'],
 d:`A man of ninety-six who told the narrator, in exchange for bootleg whiskey, how Obed Marsh brought the Deep Ones to Innsmouth and how the town died in 1846. Something out upon the water ended the conversation, and he was not seen again.`},
{id:'obed',n:'Obed Marsh',c:'pp',ep:'Founder of the Esoteric Order',s:['innsmouth'],l:['deepones','dagon','eod','zadok','olmstead','innsmouth'],
 d:`An Innsmouth sea-captain who learned the Deep Ones' bargain from Walakea, a South Sea chief, and brought it home. When the town resisted in 1846, the Deep Ones came up from the reef. His gold refinery and his descendants kept Innsmouth alive.`},
{id:'peaslee',n:'Nathaniel Wingate Peaslee',c:'pp',ep:'The displaced professor',s:['shadowtime'],l:['yith','polyps','yithcity','miskatonic','pnakotic','kulten'],
 d:`A Miskatonic professor of political economy who collapsed before his class on the 14th of May, 1908, and passed five years as someone else. When his mind returned he dreamed of a vast library and an alien body. In 1935 he found the city in the Australian desert—and in its archive, a record in his own handwriting.`},
{id:'ward',n:'Charles Dexter Ward',c:'pp',ep:'The antiquarian heir',s:['cdw'],l:['curwen','willett','yogsothoth'],
 d:`A Providence youth enthralled by the past, who discovered his ancestor Joseph Curwen, recovered his papers, and raised him from his "essential Saltes." Curwen slew him and took his place, and the family committed the altered young man as insane.`},
{id:'curwen',n:'Joseph Curwen',c:'pp',ep:'The wizard of Pawtuxet',s:['cdw'],l:['ward','willett','yogsothoth'],
 d:`A Salem necromancer who fled to Providence in 1692, and remained unnaturally young for decades while ships brought him bodies and screams sounded from his Pawtuxet farm. The townsmen raided it in 1771. Resurrected by his descendant, he murdered Ward and wore his identity, until Dr. Willett returned him to dust.`,
 q:`Doe not call up Any that you can not put downe.`,qs:'cdw'},
{id:'willett',n:'Marinus Bicknell Willett',c:'pp',ep:'The family physician',s:['cdw'],l:['ward','curwen'],
 d:`The Wards' physician, who followed Charles's case to the Pawtuxet bungalow and went down into Curwen's catacombs. He perceived who the man in the asylum truly was, and turned Curwen's own formula against him.`},
{id:'gilman',n:'Walter Gilman',c:'pp',ep:'The mathematics student',s:['witch'],l:['keziah','brownjenkin','nyarlathotep','azathoth','elderthings','arkham','miskatonic','eibon','kulten'],
 d:`A Miskatonic student from Haverhill who read the Necronomicon beside non-Euclidean calculus and quantum physics, and took Keziah Mason's old garret. Its angles let him dream his way into other dimensions, where Keziah, Brown Jenkin and the Black Man were waiting. He did not long survive Walpurgis Night.`},
{id:'keziah',n:'Keziah Mason',c:'pp',ep:'The witch of Arkham',s:['witch'],l:['brownjenkin','gilman','nyarlathotep','azathoth','arkham'],
 d:`An Arkham woman tried for witchcraft in 1692, who vanished from Salem gaol leaving curves and angles daubed upon the cell walls. She had learned how lines and angles lead out of space. In 1932 she was still at work.`},
{id:'pickman',n:'Richard Upton Pickman',c:'pp',ep:'The ghoul painter',s:['pickman','kadath'],l:['ghouls','carter'],
 d:`A Boston painter of horribly convincing ghouls, descended from a Salem woman hanged in 1692. His North End studio stood above old tunnels. His friend Thurber took a photograph off one canvas, and later looked at it. In the Dream-Quest, Pickman has become a ghoul himself, and aids Carter.`,
 q:`it was a photograph from life.`,qs:'pickman'},
{id:'zann',n:'Erich Zann',c:'pp',ep:'The mute violist',s:['zann'],l:[],
 d:`A mute old German violist in a cheap theatre orchestra, who lived at the top of a house in the Rue d'Auseil. By night he played wild music at the one window that looked out over the slope, holding something at bay. The narrator found him still playing after he had died, and has never been able to find the street again.`},
{id:'blake',n:'Robert Harrison Blake',c:'pp',ep:'The writer on College Hill',s:['haunter'],l:['trapezohedron','starrywisdom','nyarlathotep','azathoth','yogsothoth','eibon','kulten'],
 d:`A young Milwaukee writer of weird fiction who took rooms in Providence and grew fascinated by a black church on Federal Hill. Within it he found the Shining Trapezohedron and woke the thing that cannot bear light. He was found dead at his window in August 1935, after a storm had put out the city's lights. Lovecraft wrote the tale for Robert Bloch.`},
{id:'derby',n:'Edward Pickman Derby',c:'pp',ep:'The poet',s:['doorstep'],l:['asenath','upton','shoggoths','innsmouth','miskatonic'],
 d:`A sheltered child prodigy and decadent poet who married Asenath Waite. She began to take over his body for longer and longer spells. He killed her and buried her in the cellar, and she took him over from the grave.`},
{id:'asenath',n:'Asenath Waite',c:'pp',ep:'Ephraim Waite, in his daughter',s:['doorstep'],l:['derby','upton','innsmouth','deepones'],
 d:`A hypnotic, bulging-eyed Innsmouth girl who studied magic at Miskatonic and married Edward Derby. She was in truth her father, the wizard Ephraim Waite, who had exchanged bodies with her before "dying" mad. And then he wanted Derby's body.`},
{id:'upton',n:'Daniel Upton',c:'pp',ep:'The narrator who fired six shots',s:['doorstep'],l:['derby','asenath','arkham'],
 d:`An Arkham architect and Derby's oldest friend. A small, rotting thing in Derby's coat delivered a note to his door; and Upton went to the Arkham Sanitarium and shot the man in Edward's body.`,
 q:`It is true that I have sent six bullets through the head of my best friend`,qs:'doorstep'},
{id:'nahum',n:'Nahum Gardner',c:'pp',ep:'Farmer of the blasted heath',s:['colour'],l:['colour','ammi','blastedheath'],
 d:`The farmer west of Arkham whose land received the meteorite. Over a single year he watched his crops, his stock, his wife and his sons fail one by one. When Ammi Pierce found him at the last, he was crumbling away.`},
{id:'ammi',n:'Ammi Pierce',c:'pp',ep:'The neighbour who remembers',s:['colour'],l:['nahum','colour','blastedheath'],
 d:`Nahum's old neighbour, the only one who kept on visiting the farm. Decades afterward he tells the surveyor what befell, and he will not drink from the new reservoir.`},
{id:'delapore',n:'Delapore',c:'pp',ep:'Last of the de la Poers',s:['rats'],l:['exham'],
 d:`An American who restored his family's ancestral seat in 1923. His cats heard rats swarming in the walls. He led an expedition beneath the priory into a vast grotto where his forebears had kept human cattle, and was found in the dark crouched over the half-eaten body of Captain Norrys, gibbering in older tongues.`},
{id:'jermyn',n:'Arthur Jermyn',c:'pp',ep:'Heir of the Jermyns',s:['jermyn'],l:[],
 d:`Descendant of the explorer Sir Wade Jermyn, of a family strange in body and mind for generations. When a box arrived from the Congo holding the remains of Wade's mysterious wife, Arthur saw what she had been, drenched himself in oil, and set himself alight.`},
{id:'kuranes',n:'Kuranes',c:'pp',ep:'King of Celephaïs',s:['celephais','kadath'],l:['celephais','carter'],
 d:`A London dreamer who found Celephaïs in the dreams of his childhood and spent his life seeking to return. His waking body died a tramp's death; his dreaming self reigns in Celephaïs. In the Dream-Quest, sick for home, he has built a copy of a Cornish manor, and counsels Carter.`},
{id:'barzai',n:'Barzai the Wise',c:'pp',ep:'Who climbed Hatheg-Kla',s:['othergods'],l:['atal','greatones','pnakotic','ulthar'],
 d:`A sage steeped in the Pnakotic Manuscripts, who climbed Hatheg-Kla to see the gods of earth at their dancing. He found the Other Gods guarding them, and was drawn screaming into the sky. A vast carven symbol was found where he had stood.`},
{id:'atal',n:'Atal',c:'pp',ep:'Patriarch of Ulthar',s:['ulthar','othergods','kadath'],l:['barzai','cats','carter','ulthar','greatones'],
 d:`As an innkeeper's boy he saw the caravan of Menes and the vengeance of the cats. As a young man he climbed Hatheg-Kla with Barzai and came down alone. Three hundred years old in the Dream-Quest, he gives Carter wine and counsel in the temple of the Elder Ones.`},
{id:'rogers',n:'George Rogers',c:'pp',ep:'Proprietor of the waxworks',s:['museum'],l:['rhantegoth','yogsothoth'],
 d:`A former sculptor at Madame Tussaud's who opened a museum of horrors in Southwark. He claimed to have brought Rhan-Tegoth back from Alaska. When Stephen Jones's night in the museum went amiss, Rogers ended as one of his own exhibits.`},
{id:'zamacona',n:'Pánfilo de Zamacona',c:'pp',ep:'The conquistador underground',s:['mound'],l:['kenyan','tsathoggua','yig'],
 d:`A Spanish gentleman of Coronado's expedition of 1541, who followed a guide into the underground world of K'n-yan. He lived there for years, fled with a woman named T'la-yub, and left his manuscript within the Oklahoma mound.`},
{id:'tyog',n:"T'yog",c:'pp',ep:'High priest of Shub-Niggurath',s:['aeons'],l:['ghatanothoa','shub'],
 d:`A priest of Mu who wrote a protective scroll with Shub-Niggurath's aid, and climbed Yaddith-Gho to challenge Ghatanothoa. The god's priests had replaced his scroll with a counterfeit. Turned to stone with his brain still living, he ended as a mummy in Boston's Cabot Museum.`},
{id:'outsider',n:'The Outsider',c:'pp',ep:'The nameless narrator',s:['outsider'],l:['ghouls'],
 d:`A being reared alone in a dark and crumbling castle. He climbed the tower toward the light and walked into a revel, where the guests fled screaming. Reaching for the monster in a gilded frame, he touched cold glass. Now he rides the night-wind with the ghouls.`,
 q:`I know always that I am an outsider; a stranger in this century and among those who are still men.`,qs:'outsider'},

/* Cults */
{id:'cthulhucult',n:'The Cthulhu Cult',c:'cu',ep:'Worldwide and undying',s:['coc'],l:['cthulhu','castro','legrasse','angell','johansen'],
 d:`A cult that waits for the stars to come right, reaching from the Greenland tribe Professor Webb encountered to Legrasse's swamp-worshippers and the crew of the Alert. Old Castro says its deathless leaders dwell in the mountains of China.`,
 q:`Iä! Iä! Cthulhu fhtagn!`,qs:'coc'},
{id:'eod',n:'Esoteric Order of Dagon',c:'cu',ep:'The church of Innsmouth',s:['innsmouth'],l:['obed','dagon','hydra','deepones','innsmouth','zadok','olmstead'],
 d:`The faith Obed Marsh brought back from the South Seas, which took over Innsmouth's old Masonic Hall. Its oaths bind its members to Dagon and the Deep Ones. Federal agents broke it up in 1928.`},
{id:'starrywisdom',n:'Church of Starry Wisdom',c:'cu',ep:'The sect on Federal Hill',s:['haunter'],l:['trapezohedron','blake','nyarlathotep'],
 d:`Founded in 1844 by Professor Enoch Bowen after he brought the Shining Trapezohedron back from Egypt, the sect met in a black stone church upon Federal Hill in Providence. Disappearances followed it until it was suppressed in 1877. The steeple stayed sealed until Robert Blake went in.`},

/* Places */
{id:'rlyeh',n:"R'lyeh",c:'pl',dw:'919.9',ep:'47°9′ S, 126°43′ W',s:['coc','innsmouth'],l:['cthulhu','starspawn','johansen','cthulhucult'],
 d:`Cthulhu's tomb-city, built by his spawn and sunk beneath the South Pacific. Its geometry is all wrong: one sailor was swallowed by an angle of masonry which was acute, but behaved as if it were obtuse. It rose for a few days in 1925.`},
{id:'yhanthlei',n:"Y'ha-nthlei",c:'pl',dw:'919.9',ep:'The city beyond Devil Reef',s:['innsmouth'],l:['deepones','dagon','hydra','olmstead','innsmouth'],
 d:`The Deep Ones' cyclopean and many-columned city in the abyss beyond Devil Reef, off Innsmouth. Federal submarines torpedoed the deeps in 1928, but the city survived.`},
{id:'arkham',n:'Arkham',c:'pl',ep:'The witch-haunted town',s:['west','unnamable','silverkey','colour','dunwich','whisperer','mountains','innsmouth','witch','doorstep','shadowtime'],l:['miskatonic','keziah','gilman','west','carter','upton','kingsport','innsmouth','dunwich','brownjenkin'],
 d:`A fictional town upon the Miskatonic River in Massachusetts, thronged with gambrel roofs, ancient burying-grounds and memories of the witch-trials. Home to Miskatonic University, the Witch House, the Arkham Sanitarium, and many of Lovecraft's narrators.`},
{id:'miskatonic',n:'Miskatonic University',c:'pl',ep:"Arkham's university",s:['west','dunwich','whisperer','mountains','witch','doorstep','shadowtime'],l:['armitage','wilmarth','dyer','lake','peaslee','west','gilman','asenath','necronomicon','arkham','colour'],
 d:`Its library keeps one of the few complete Necronomicons under lock and key. Its faculty keep stumbling upon the Mythos: Armitage, Wilmarth, Dyer, Lake, Peaslee. Among its students are Herbert West, Walter Gilman and Asenath Waite.`},
{id:'innsmouth',n:'Innsmouth',c:'pl',ep:'The shadowed port',s:['innsmouth','doorstep'],l:['deepones','obed','zadok','eod','yhanthlei','asenath','olmstead'],
 d:`A decayed seaport near Arkham, sagging and half-deserted, with a gold refinery, a reek of fish, and the hall of the Order. Many of its people bear the "Innsmouth look." Federal agents raided it in the winter of 1927–28 and dynamited whole rows of waterfront houses.`},
{id:'dunwich',n:'Dunwich',c:'pl',ep:'The decadent hill town',s:['dunwich'],l:['wilbur','twin','sentinelhill','oldwhateley'],
 d:`A decaying village in the upper Miskatonic valley, ringed by rounded hills crowned with circles of stone. The whippoorwills there are said to wait outside a dying man's window to catch his soul as it departs.`},
{id:'kingsport',n:'Kingsport',c:'pl',ep:'The ancient seaport',s:['festival','strangehouse'],l:['nodens','arkham'],
 d:`A fog-wrapped colonial seaport near Arkham, with steep lanes and a churchyard upon Central Hill. At Yuletide its old families go down beneath the church. On Kingsport Head, the loftiest cliff, stands the strange high house in the mist.`},
{id:'blastedheath',n:'The Blasted Heath',c:'pl',ep:'Five acres of grey dust',s:['colour'],l:['colour','nahum','ammi'],
 d:`What was once Nahum Gardner's farm, west of Arkham: five acres of fine grey dust where nothing grows. The new reservoir will cover it.`},
{id:'sentinelhill',n:'Sentinel Hill',c:'pl',ep:'Altar of the Whateleys',s:['dunwich'],l:['oldwhateley','twin','armitage','wilbur','dunwich'],
 d:`The highest hill near Dunwich, crowned with a table-like stone and strewn with bones. The Whateleys held rites there on May-Eve and Hallowe'en, and Armitage destroyed the twin there.`},
{id:'exham',n:'Exham Priory',c:'pl',dw:'914.2',ep:'Seat of the de la Poers',s:['rats'],l:['delapore'],
 d:`A priory near Anchester in England, built over a Druidic or older temple and a Roman shrine to Cybele. Beneath it lies the twilit grotto where generations of de la Poers kept and ate human livestock.`},
{id:'yuggoth',n:'Yuggoth',c:'pl',dw:'919.9',ep:'Pluto',s:['whisperer','haunter','museum','aeons'],l:['migo','akeley','rhantegoth','ghatanothoa','trapezohedron'],
 d:`A world at the rim of the solar system, identified with Pluto upon that planet's discovery in 1930. Its black rivers of pitch and windowless towers house the outpost of the Mi-Go; Rhan-Tegoth and Ghatanothoa came from there, and so did the Shining Trapezohedron.`},
{id:'leng',n:'Plateau of Leng',c:'pl',dw:'919.9',ep:'The cold plateau',s:['hound','kadath','mountains'],l:['lengmen','shantaks','moonbeasts','carter','antarctic'],
 d:`A cold and forbidden plateau which may lie in Central Asia, or in the Dreamlands, or in both. The jade amulet of "The Hound" came from its corpse-eating cult. Its stone villages house the horned men, and a monastery holds the High Priest Not to Be Described. Dyer wonders whether the Antarctic plateau is Leng itself.`},
{id:'kadath',n:'Unknown Kadath',c:'pl',dw:'154.63',ep:'In the cold waste',s:['othergods','kadath'],l:['greatones','nyarlathotep','carter','leng','azathoth'],
 d:`The highest mountain of the Dreamlands, hidden in the cold waste beyond Leng, where the gods of earth dwell in an onyx castle at the summit. Few dreamers have found it, and Nyarlathotep waits for those who do.`},
{id:'ulthar',n:'Ulthar',c:'pl',dw:'154.63',ep:'Beyond the river Skai',s:['ulthar','othergods','kadath'],l:['cats','atal','barzai','pnakotic','carter'],
 d:`A peaceful town of the Dreamlands where no man may kill a cat; home of old Atal and of the temple of the Elder Ones.`},
{id:'celephais',n:'Celephaïs',c:'pl',dw:'154.63',ep:'City of Ooth-Nargai',s:['celephais','kadath'],l:['kuranes','carter'],
 d:`A marble city in the valley of Ooth-Nargai beside the Cerenerian Sea, where time does not pass. Kuranes dreamed it as a child, and rules it as its king.`},
{id:'sarnath',n:'Sarnath',c:'pl',dw:'154.63',ep:'In the land of Mnar',s:['sarnath'],l:['bokrug'],
 d:`A proud city of marble and gold, built near the grey stone city of Ib. On the thousandth festival of Ib's destruction it vanished in a night; only the idol of Bokrug remained.`},
{id:'namelesscity',n:'The Nameless City',c:'pl',dw:'915.3',ep:'In the Arabian desert',s:['nameless'],l:['alhazred','necronomicon'],
 d:`A forgotten city half-buried in the Arabian sands, its chambers too low for a man to stand upright. Murals in a sloping tunnel show its reptilian builders—and they are not all dead. Abdul Alhazred dreamed of it the night before he wrote his couplet.`},
{id:'antarctic',n:'City of the Elder Things',c:'pl',dw:'919.89',ep:'Beyond the mountains of madness',s:['mountains'],l:['elderthings','shoggoths','dyer','danforth','lake','leng'],
 d:`A cyclopean city of stone tens of millions of years old, sprawled across the plateau behind an Antarctic range loftier than the Himalayas. Its carvings record the history of the Elder Things; its tunnels lead down to a black sea where shoggoths live still.`},
{id:'yithcity',n:'City of the Great Race',c:'pl',dw:'919.4',ep:'Under the Great Sandy Desert',s:['shadowtime'],l:['yith','polyps','peaslee'],
 d:`The Great Race's city in prehistoric Australia, built about an immense archive whose metal cases hold the histories written by every mind they borrowed. Its basalt blocks lie half-buried in the desert, where Peaslee went down among them in July 1935.`},

/* Tomes & artifacts */
{id:'necronomicon',n:'The Necronomicon',c:'tm',ep:'Al Azif, by Abdul Alhazred',s:['hound','festival','coc','cdw','dunwich','whisperer','mountains','witch','doorstep','shadowtime','haunter'],l:['alhazred','miskatonic','armitage','wilbur','yogsothoth','cthulhu','umr','danforth','namelesscity'],
 d:`Lovecraft's most celebrated invention. Written as Al Azif about 730, translated into Greek by Theodorus Philetas in 950 and into Latin by Olaus Wormius in 1228. Known copies repose in the British Museum, the Bibliothèque nationale, Harvard's Widener Library, the University of Buenos Aires, and Miskatonic. Lovecraft quotes it, but never sets forth its contents in full.`,
 q:`That is not dead which can eternal lie, / And with strange aeons even death may die.`,qs:'nameless'},
{id:'pnakotic',n:'The Pnakotic Manuscripts',c:'tm',ep:'Older than humanity',s:['polaris','othergods','kadath','shadowtime'],l:['yith','barzai','ulthar','peaslee'],
 d:`An ancient text from before the coming of man, known in Lomar and copied in the Dreamlands. Barzai had it by heart. Peaslee's discoveries hint that its fragments reach back to the Great Race.`},
{id:'eibon',n:'The Book of Eibon',c:'tm',ep:'Borrowed from Clark Ashton Smith',s:['witch','haunter'],l:['gilman','blake','tsathoggua'],
 d:`Smith's grimoire of the Hyperborean wizard Eibon, which Lovecraft added to his own catalogues of forbidden books. Walter Gilman and Robert Blake both study it.`},
{id:'kulten',n:'Unaussprechlichen Kulten',c:'tm',ep:'By von Junzt; borrowed from Robert E. Howard',s:['witch','shadowtime','haunter'],l:['gilman','blake','peaslee'],
 d:`Friedrich von Junzt's "Nameless Cults," invented by Howard and given its German title by Lovecraft. Gilman, Peaslee and Blake all consult it.`},
{id:'trapezohedron',n:'The Shining Trapezohedron',c:'tm',ep:'A window on all space and time',s:['haunter'],l:['starrywisdom','blake','nyarlathotep','yuggoth'],
 d:`A nearly black, red-striated polyhedron held by seven supports within an asymmetrical metal box. It was fashioned on Yuggoth, treasured by the pharaoh Nephren-Ka, and brought to Providence by Enoch Bowen in 1844. To gaze into it is to summon the Haunter of the Dark, which cannot survive the light.`},
{id:'silverkey',n:'The Silver Key',c:'tm',ep:"Carter's inheritance",s:['silverkey','gate'],l:['carter','umr','yogsothoth'],
 d:`A great, tarnished, arabesqued key in an ancestral box, with a parchment in an unknown tongue. It bore Carter back to his boyhood and out of the waking world. In Through the Gates it unlocks the Ultimate Gate itself.`},
];

